A new standard-library module, `Std.Axioms`, is the one place for trusted facts that are true of Dafny's intended model but cannot be proved from its axiomatization. Each is an `{:axiom}` lemma without a body, so its conclusions are available only where it is called, and `dafny audit` reports it; a program that does not call one is unaffected. `Std.Axioms.FunctionExtensionality` states function extensionality for total arrows (`->`) on `int`, `real`, `bool`, `ORDINAL`, pairs of `int` and the empty domain: two such functions that agree at every argument are equal. It is not stated for an arbitrary domain, which would be unsound (a subset type such as `nat` is enough to prove `false`), nor for partial (`-->`) or heap-reading (`~>`) arrows. `Std.Axioms.Reals.Complete` is the completeness of `real`, moved from `Std.RealCompleteness`, whose `Complete` is now a verified call of it with the same interface.
